  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nScattered thunderstorms have already developed early this\nafternoon across western MN. One storm that promoted Severe \nThunderstorm Warnings was an elevated supercell thanks to \nMUCAPE of 500-1000 J/kg and 35 kts of effective bulk shear.\nExtensive stratus and fog across most of central and western MN\nhas kept temperatures stuck in the 50s, whereas farther south \nand east temperatures are approaching 70 where skies cleared \nearly this morning. As the short wave continues east this \nafternoon, additional development is expected across the warm \nsector. Elevated storms have proven capable of becoming well \nestablished across western MN already. The question is not \nwhether we will get widespread storms, but whether they will \nbecome surface-based and therefore capable of tornadoes. Low \nlevel instability is weak due to neutral lapse rates and \nmarginal surface moisture. Also, the widespread nature of the \ndevelopment may further negatively impact any available low \nlevel instability. Bottom line, expect storms and be aware of a \ncouple that may pose a threat for tornadoes, large hail, and\ndamaging winds, especially if the storms remain somewhat \ndiscrete. This remains a conditional threat.\n\nHigh pressure will build in for the rest of the week, then\nsettle over the East Coast this weekend. Return flow will bring\na chance of showers Friday night and Saturday. A larger scale \nsystem still looks to take shape over the Rockies this weekend\nand then pull across the Plains early next week. Some guidance\ncuts the system off over the Plains with a large precipitation\nshield to the north and east of the center. Others are more\nprogressive. Regardless how it eventually unfolds, more rain\nshould be coming next week.",
